Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> archmage84 schrieb:
>
>> They can be used for a ranged attack (a plus), but they have bad
>> damage,
>
> Depends on the actual polearm; a bardiche does 7.5 avg damage
> against large monsters.

There's more variation of weight than damage among pole arm types.  Even
though the halberd gives the most damage I think spetum is the best
compromise for a game I wish to use them.

>> and can't be #twoweaponed (two minuses). Anything else to
>> consider? Maybe the frequency of generation?

At skilled a pole arm can be used to the knight's jump location not just
straight on.  That's a good option to have when at choke point of either
of the two castles.  There's a lot less chance of getting hit by wand of
missile shots.  Blast the monsters as they advance into the choke
square.  The problem is when the first one survives that first whack and
advances to melee with you.  The time it takes to switch to a different
melee weapon allows other monsters in.  At some point your quiver will
empty then a monster will get through.  But a lot of monsters can be
killed in relative safety.

Is it worth the effort to exercise and enhande the skill before entering
either castle? I've tried it a few times and I no longer think so.  But
a knight who has advanced lance to at least skilled for other reasons
should use the tactic.

> Besides random generation; trolls come equipped with polearms.

And spetum is among the 4 types of pole arms they get generated with. 
That's a lot of chances to get a spetum that's not cursed.  The limit on
pole arms isn't the availability; it's the price of the skill slots
versus the few times it's a big enough advantage.


